issue.body:
It happens when switching to higher bitrate on Nexus 10 or Samsung device only (so far). The root cause is due to different implementation of the codec, with combination of ExoPlayer code.
# Root Cause
1. It only happens on Samsung devices (so far), or device that use the Exynos chip. Reproducible on Nexus 10.
2. The following line of code may cause putting a data bigger than the ByteBuffer.remaining() that cause the BufferOverflowException: 
   https://github.com/google/ExoPlayer/blob/dev/library/src/main/java/com/google/android/exoplayer/extractor/RollingSampleBuffer.java#L284

   ```
   int toCopy = Math.min(remaining, allocationLength - positionInAllocation);

   // when target.remaining() < toCopy --> BufferOverflowException thrown
   target.put(allocation.data, allocation.translateOffset(positionInAllocation), toCopy);
   ```
3. The codec.configure result differently on Qualcom chip vs Exynos. Qualcom will always return the max  capacity when code.getInputBuffers called, as opposed to Exynos return based on the width x height passed via MediaFormat.
   https://github.com/google/ExoPlayer/blob/dev/library/src/main/java/com/google/android/exoplayer/MediaCodecTrackRenderer.java#L366

   ```
   configureCodec(codec, decoderName, format.getFrameworkMediaFormatV16(), mediaCrypto);
   ...
   inputBuffers = codec.getInputBuffers();
   ```
## Based on my testing:

**Non Samsung device with video codec: OMX.qcom.video.decoder.avc**
- HTC One M8, always returns input buffer with size 7086080
- Nexus 5, always returns input buffer with size 11059200

**Samsung devices with video codec: OMX.Exynos.AVC.Decoder**
- Input buffer size 149760, with MediaFormat:

  > {csd-1=java.nio.ByteArrayBuffer[position=0,limit=8,capacity=8], mime=video/avc, max-height=576, com.google.android.videos.pixelWidthHeightRatio=1.0, height=**234**, width=**416**, csd-0=java.nio.ByteArrayBuffer[position=0,limit=36,capacity=36], max-width=1024}
- Input buffer size 497664, with MediaFormat:

  > {csd-1=java.nio.ByteArrayBuffer[position=0,limit=8,capacity=8], mime=video/avc, max-height=360, com.google.android.videos.pixelWidthHeightRatio=1.0, height=**432**, width=**768**, csd-0=java.nio.ByteArrayBuffer[position=0,limit=38,capacity=38], max-width=640}
# Solution

Passing the maximum resolution (width and height) that was available on stream list.

https://github.com/google/ExoPlayer/blob/dev/library/src/main/java/com/google/android/exoplayer/MediaFormat.java#L146

```
// remove the final keyword on width and heigh attribute
public void setMaxVideoDimensions(int maxWidth, int maxHeight) {
    this.width = maxWidth;
    this.height = maxHeight;
    this.maxWidth = maxWidth;
    this.maxHeight = maxHeight;
    if (frameworkMediaFormat != null) {
      maybeSetMaxDimensionsV16(frameworkMediaFormat);
    }
  }
```
